<LegalPage title="Community Guidelines" kicker="For artists and venues">
<p class="lede">Think about the last time you walked into a coffee shop and there was art on the wall. Somebody made it. Somebody decided to hang it. And you got to decide what you thought of it. That’s the whole idea here, and these guidelines exist to protect it.</p>

<h3>What belongs here</h3>
<p>Art made to be looked at: painting, drawing, printmaking, photography, collage, mixed media, sculpture, and fiber and textile art made for the wall, like art quilts, weaving, and embroidery. Ceramics and glass count when they’re made for display rather than for use.</p>

<h3>What doesn’t</h3>
<p>Crafts, useful objects, and merchandise: jewelry, cutting boards, mugs, printed T-shirts, and your art printed onto products. There are good places for those things. This just isn’t one of them.</p>

<h3>The gray areas</h3>
<p>And here’s where it gets complicated. A bowl nobody will ever eat out of. A carved spoon that’s really a sculpture of a spoon. A necklace built as a wall piece. We ask one question: was it made to be looked at? If you’re not sure, apply anyway and tell us about it. A person will look, and we’ll give you a straight answer.</p>

<h3>Art made with AI</h3>
<p>It’s welcome here, with a label. Check “Made with AI” on any piece where an AI tool generated the image or a real part of it. The piece then gets a small “Made with AI” tag everywhere it appears. Ordinary editing of a photo of your physical work, like cropping, color correction, or removing the background, doesn’t need the label. An AI piece that isn’t labeled can be hidden until it is.</p>

<h3>Nudity, violence, and hard subjects</h3>
<p>They’re allowed, and they don’t come with warnings.</p>
<p>Artists have painted bodies and violence for as long as there’s been art. Goya etched the <em>Disasters of War</em>. Every museum you’ve been in has a room of nudes. Nobody hangs a warning label on those, and we’re not going to start.</p>
<p>Here’s the other reason. This site points people to walls, and every wall belongs to somebody. Whether a piece hangs in a café where kids eat pancakes is a decision for the venue and the artist to make together. They know the room, and we don’t. So the site doesn’t second-guess it.</p>

<h3>Where we do draw lines</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Nothing sexual involving anyone under 18.</strong> Ever. It comes down immediately and gets reported to the authorities.</li>
  <li><strong>No intimate images of real, identifiable people without their consent.</strong></li>
  <li><strong>No work that isn’t yours</strong>, and nothing that copies someone else’s work without permission.</li>
  <li><strong>No art used as a weapon against real people.</strong> Art can depict ugly things, including hate. It can’t be a vehicle for threatening, harassing, or exposing real people, or for attacking people because of who they are.</li>
  <li><strong>No private information about other people</strong>, like home addresses or phone numbers.</li>
  <li><strong>No spam, ads, or fake showings.</strong></li>
  <li><strong>Nothing illegal.</strong></li>
</ul>

<h3>Be decent to people</h3>
<p>You’re allowed to be a jerk in your art. Not to people. That goes for how artists, venues, and visitors treat each other in messages and in person.</p>

<h3>How reports work</h3>
<p>Anyone can tap “Report this” under a piece. A real person reads every report. Most of the time the piece stays up while we look. If a report holds up, we hide the piece and email the artist the reason. If it doesn’t, we close the report and nothing changes. Either way, the artist can write to us, and we’ll listen.</p>

<h3>If someone posted your work</h3>
<p>Tap “Report this” under the piece, or follow the steps in our <a href="/copyright/">Copyright</a> page for a formal notice. A complete formal notice gets the piece taken down.</p>
</LegalPage>
